    "<div data-citation-items=\"%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F60638%2Fitems%2FMFKAU4GY%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F60638%2Fitems%2FMFKAU4GY%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22webpage%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22Focus%20on%20lowering%20during%20your%20lifts%20to%20reach%20new%20strength%20and%20muscle%20heights.%22%2C%22citation-key%22%3A%22Thieme22eccentricOverloadTrain%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22Men's%20Health%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en-US%22%2C%22note%22%3A%22section%3A%20Fitness%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Eccentric%20Overload%20Training%20Focuses%20on%20Lowering%20to%20Reach%20New%20Heights%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fwww.menshealth.com%2Ffitness%2Fa41019264%2Feccentric-overload-training%2F%22%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Thieme%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Trevor%22%7D%5D%2C%22accessed%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222026%22%2C7%2C6%5D%5D%7D%2C%22issued%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222022%22%2C8%2C29%5D%5D%7D%7D%7D%2C%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F60638%2Fitems%2FUKBC74VT%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F60638%2Fitems%2FUKBC74VT%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22article-journal%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22Muscles%20are%20significant%20contributors%20to%20the%20high%20joint%20forces%20developed%20in%20the%20knee%20during%20human%20walking.%20Not%20only%20do%20muscles%20contribute%20to%20the%20knee%20joint%20forces%20by%20acting%20to%20compress%20the%20joint%2C%20but%20they%20also%20develop%20joint%20forces%20indirectly%20through%20their%20contributions%20to%20the%20ground%20reaction%20forces%20via%20dynamic%20coupling.%20Thus%2C%20muscles%20can%20have%20significant%20contributions%20to%20forces%20at%20joints%20they%20do%20not%20span.%20However%2C%20few%20studies%20have%20investigated%20how%20the%20major%20lower-limb%20muscles%20contribute%20to%20the%20knee%20joint%20contact%20forces%20during%20walking.%20The%20goal%20of%20this%20study%20was%20to%20use%20a%20muscle-actuated%20forward%20dynamics%20simulation%20of%20walking%20to%20identify%20how%20individual%20muscles%20contribute%20to%20the%20axial%20tibio-femoral%20joint%20force.%20The%20simulation%20results%20showed%20that%20the%20vastii%20muscles%20are%20the%20primary%20contributors%20to%20the%20axial%20joint%20force%20in%20early%20stance%20while%20the%20gastrocnemius%20is%20the%20primary%20contributor%20in%20late%20stance.%20The%20tibio-femoral%20joint%20force%20generated%20by%20these%20muscles%20was%20at%20times%20greater%20than%20the%20muscle%20forces%20themselves.%20Muscles%20that%20do%20not%20cross%20the%20knee%20joint%20(e.g.%2C%20the%20gluteus%20maximus%20and%20soleus)%20also%20have%20significant%20contributions%20to%20the%20tibio-femoral%20joint%20force%20through%20their%20contributions%20to%20the%20ground%20reaction%20forces.%20Further%2C%20small%20changes%20in%20walking%20kinematics%20(e.g.%2C%20knee%20flexion%20angle)%20can%20have%20a%20significant%20effect%20on%20the%20magnitude%20of%20the%20knee%20joint%20forces.%20Thus%2C%20altering%20walking%20mechanics%20and%20muscle%20coordination%20patterns%20to%20utilize%20muscle%20groups%20that%20perform%20the%20same%20biomechanical%20function%2C%20yet%20contribute%20less%20to%20the%20knee%20joint%20forces%20may%20be%20an%20effective%20way%20to%20reduce%20knee%20joint%20loading%20during%20walking.%22%2C%22citation-key%22%3A%22Sasaki10muscleContribKneeFrc%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22Journal%20of%20biomechanics%22%2C%22DOI%22%3A%2210.1016%2Fj.jbiomech.2010.06.011%22%2C%22ISSN%22%3A%220021-9290%22%2C%22issue%22%3A%2214%22%2C%22journalAbbreviation%22%3A%22J%20Biomech%22%2C%22page%22%3A%222780-2784%22%2C%22PMID%22%3A%2220655046%22%2C%22PMCID%22%3A%22PMC2963724%22%2C%22source%22%3A%22PubMed%20Central%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Individual%20Muscle%20Contributions%20to%20the%20Axial%20Knee%20Joint%20Contact%20Force%20during%20Normal%20Walking%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fwww.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2